comments.body:
Some relevant information from the HELP ticket:

[~bruce.lucas] says:

{quote}
Using the above info I was able to put together a very simple repro against a standalone mongod which eliminates considerations of timestamp management, lag, flow control, etc. Attached as repro.sh.

It consists of 20 threads each inserting 100 k tiny documents with two secondary unique indexes. Expected behavior (observed in 4.2) is that insert rate should be steady and each thread should complete at about the same time. Observed behavior in 4.4 is that insert rate is erratic, and a couple of the threads typically seem to get stuck apparently indefinitely with a high rate of missed history store reads with stacks like the above.
{quote}

And ..

{quote}
The problem exists in 4.4.0, but it seems there may have been a change in behavior between 4.4.1 and 4.4.2 that could be informative:

In 4.4.2 the "reads missed" begins immediately, and at A we have a couple writers stuck seemingly indefinitely:
{quote}
!442.png|width=100%!
{quote}
Whereas in 4.4.1 the "reads missed" does not begin immediately. At A we have one writer thread left that's stuck, but "reads missed" does not start until B, so it looks like we were stuck for some reason that didn't initially trigger "reads missed":
{quote}
!441.png|width=100%!

Update:
 There's a key difference between when we reproduce this issue and when we don't, as with my reproduce it repros ~60% of the time. The difference is visualized in the FTDC below:

!image-2021-03-31-17-11-35-375.png!

We can see that the cache_pages_written from cache correlates with the history store table reads missed statistic rising, and if more FTDC was shown the inevitable long hang of the one writer thread. Additionally with this FTDC I have a series of log messages that lead me to the following theory:
 * All the threads are performing search_near calls in {{_keyExists}} for a given key. While this is happening the page gets evicted due to forced eviction being performed by an application thread. I think this is the thread that likely encounters the issue.
 * The search near call is calling next and prev a LOT because nothing is visible to it due to transaction id visibility checks. (I'm still not 100% sure about this as I'm not too sure how long the opCtx transaction is around for).
 ** In the scenario when we haven't evicted the page yet, these prev, next calls are on the insert list and reasonably quick so we don't notice them.
 ** In the scenario when the page is now evicted, we do a {{__wt_txn_read}}, get the page from disk, crack the cell, find nothing (as nothing is visible to our transaction), then search the history store, again find nothing. For each next / prev call.

This behaviour is expected, however I'm sure there's a way we could do better. I'll continue to investigate, I was able to stop this from reproducing with the following patch:
{noformat}
diff --git a/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_read.c b/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_read.c
index ab91af5b21..90336fc33c 100644
--- a/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_read.c
+++ b/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_read.c
@@ -22,6 +22,8 @@ __evict_force_check(WT_SESSION_IMPL *session, WT_REF *ref)
     btree = S2BT(session);
     page = ref->page;
+    return (false);
+
     /* Leaf pages only. */
     if (F_ISSET(ref, WT_REF_FLAG_INTERNAL))
         return (false); {noformat}
 My cut down reproducer script is:
{noformat}
./build/install/bin/mongo --quiet --eval '

    load("jstests/libs/parallelTester.js")


    db.c.createIndex({a: 1}, {unique: true})

    nthreads = 10
    threads = []

    for (var t=0; t<nthreads; t++) {
        thread = new Thread(function(t) {
            count = 5000
            every = 200
            for (var i = 0; i < count; i += every) {
                many = []
                for (j = 0; j < every; j++) {
                    inx = t * count + i + j
                    many.push({_id: inx, a: inx, b: inx})
                }
                db.c.insertMany(many)
                if (i%10000==0) print(t, i)
            }
            print("thread", t, "finished")
        }, t)
        threads.push(thread)
        thread.start()
    }
    for (var t = 0; t < nthreads; t++)
        threads[t].join()
'
 {noformat}

I've attempted both potential workarounds, the first one, which isn't a real fix does resolve a large portion of the regression. The second one, i.e. the add a prefix to the prefixes, doesn't work as well as it should which is unexpected. Each transaction is inserting 200 records, and removing 200 records and then search_nearing for 200 records assuming I'm right about how transactions work in the index building phase. As such the thread that is getting caught in the search near loop should reasonably be able too see an increasing amount of records whenever it does a search near.

My prefix patch is as follows:
{noformat}
diff --git a/src/mongo/db/storage/wiredtiger/wiredtiger_index.cpp b/src/mongo/db/storage/wiredtiger/wiredtiger_index.cpp
index 87bda0ace9..7afd62c6b8 100644
--- a/src/mongo/db/storage/wiredtiger/wiredtiger_index.cpp
+++ b/src/mongo/db/storage/wiredtiger/wiredtiger_index.cpp
@@ -1494,8 +1494,10 @@ Status WiredTigerIndexUnique::_insert(OperationContext* opCtx,
         // should be unique.
         auto sizeWithoutRecordId =
             KeyString::sizeWithoutRecordIdAtEnd(keyString.getBuffer(), keyString.getSize());
-        WiredTigerItem prefixKeyItem(keyString.getBuffer(), sizeWithoutRecordId);

+        std::string new_prefix = "AAAAAAAA" + std::string(keyString.getBuffer()).substr(0, sizeWithoutRecordId);
+        //.WiredTigerItem prefixKeyItem(keyString.getBuffer(), sizeWithoutRecordId);
+        WiredTigerItem prefixKeyItem(new_prefix.c_str(), new_prefix.size());
         // First phase inserts the prefix key to prohibit concurrent insertions of same key
         setKey(c, prefixKeyItem.Get());
         c->set_value(c, emptyItem.Get());
 {noformat}

The next work here is to do 2 things:
# Determine why only one thread seems to get stuck in this loop.
# Determine why despite there being visible records in later batches we still seem to get caught looking for records that we can't see.

The "loop" here appears to be quirk of search_near and a known issue with the WiredTiger API. Search near attempts to return the record itself if its a direct match or the logically adjacent record, which is either the smallest record larger than the key or the largest record smaller than the key. Now as we're searching for the prefix, we won't place on the exact record as there won't be an exact match. So we're guaranteed to a next and then a prev loop, however nothing is visible so those loops traverse the entire tree. As such for every failed search near we're doing a very large number of next and prev calls internally. But we should still be able to know that the key we've prev'd or next'd to isn't within our desired range and exit this loop earlier than we do.

So I've found the cause of the regression in performance, it was a change introduced by WT-5461. Which instead of returning a value to the caller simply nexted or preved as nothing was visible. This is a deviation from 4.2 which would return the value back to the caller even if nothing was visible on the update chain as the assumption is that the on disk value is visible.The original change fixed a test failure so I'll need to understand what that failure was and how I can fix this without simply removing the fix introduced by WT-5461. 

With this patch the full reproducer posted by [~bruce.lucas] runs at the same speed or even quicker than 4.2.:
{noformat}
diff --git a/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_curnext.c b/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_curnext.c
index 18fdef7331..068a5097cb 100644
--- a/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_curnext.c
+++ b/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_curnext.c
@@ -404,10 +404,6 @@ restart_read_page:
         WT_RET(__cursor_row_slot_key_return(cbt, rip, &kpack, &kpack_used));
         WT_RET(__wt_txn_read(
           session, cbt, &cbt->iface.key, WT_RECNO_OOB, WT_ROW_UPDATE(page, rip), NULL));
-        if (cbt->upd_value->type == WT_UPDATE_INVALID) {
-            ++*skippedp;
-            continue;
-        }
         if (cbt->upd_value->type == WT_UPDATE_TOMBSTONE) {
             if (cbt->upd_value->tw.stop_txn != WT_TXN_NONE &&
               __wt_txn_upd_value_visible_all(session, cbt->upd_value))
diff --git a/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_curprev.c b/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_curprev.c
index 7517eac77d..c66a264d8b 100644
--- a/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_curprev.c
+++ b/src/third_party/wiredtiger/src/btree/bt_curprev.c
@@ -555,10 +555,6 @@ restart_read_page:
         WT_RET(__cursor_row_slot_key_return(cbt, rip, &kpack, &kpack_used));
         WT_RET(__wt_txn_read(
           session, cbt, &cbt->iface.key, WT_RECNO_OOB, WT_ROW_UPDATE(page, rip), NULL));
-        if (cbt->upd_value->type == WT_UPDATE_INVALID) {
-            ++*skippedp;
-            continue;
-        }
         if (cbt->upd_value->type == WT_UPDATE_TOMBSTONE) {
             if (cbt->upd_value->tw.stop_txn != WT_TXN_NONE &&
               __wt_txn_upd_value_visible_all(session, cbt->upd_value))
{noformat}

I've pushed up a draft PR for this. The failing test is: {{profile_operation_metrics.js}} which appears to be expecting specific values logged to the profiler for certain operations. The failing scenario is insertion of a second key into a unique index. I'm attempting to understand why and am going to use gdbserver tomorrow to see if that allows me to debug resmoke tests easily. 

The command to run the failing test is:
{noformat}
buildscripts/resmoke.py run --storageEngine=wiredTiger jstests/noPassthrough/profile_operation_metrics.js
{noformat}

The relevant profile increment happens on line 111 and line 972 of {{wiredtiger_index.cpp}}.
